{"title":"Adding fractions with different denominators","key_idea":"To add fractions with different bottom numbers, you must make the bottom numbers the same before adding the tops.","method":["Find the smallest number that both denominators divide into evenly.","Change each fraction to an equal fraction with that new common denominator.","Add the numerators and keep the common denominator."],"worked_example":{"problem":"Add 1/3 and 1/6.","steps":["The denominators are 3 and 6; the smallest number both divide into is 6.","Change 1/3 to an equal fraction with denominator 6: multiply top and bottom by 2 to get 2/6.","The fraction 1/6 already has denominator 6, so it stays the same.","Add the numerators: 2/6 + 1/6 = 3/6.","Simplify 3/6 to 1/2."],"answer":"1/2"},"key_terms":[{"term":"common denominator","meaning":"a number that both denominators divide into evenly"},{"term":"equivalent fraction","meaning":"a fraction that looks different but has the same value"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding the top and bottom numbers together instead of only adding the tops: for example, writing 3/4 + 1/8 = 4/12.","Forgetting to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the same number when making an equivalent fraction."],"remember":"Make the bottoms the same, then add only the tops; the bottom stays the same.","quick_check":[{"q":"Add 1/2 and 1/4. What is the common denominator?","a":"4"},{"q":"Add 2/5 and 1/10. What is your answer?","a":"1/2 or 5/10"}],"tell_your_child":"First, look at the bottom numbers and find a number they both fit into evenly. Then rewrite each fraction so they both have that same bottom number, and finally add just the top numbers together."}
